    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "Aluminum is what to choose when your snow is heavy and your pavement is rough. True Temper's 20-inch aluminum combo pairs an aluminum blade with a steel wear strip, an ergonomic bent steel handle and a D-grip, according to retailer listings. When True Temper launched the design, its maker described it as able to handle 30 percent larger loads than a traditional flat aluminum shovel, because the blade is curved like a combo rather than flat like a coal shovel. An aluminum blade stays stiff in deep cold, when cheap plastic can turn brittle, and it scrapes packed snow better than poly. The wider 20-inch blade moves more per pass than an 18-inch combo. The downsides are weight and stickiness: aluminum is heavier than poly, and wet snow can cling to it unless you coat the blade with a silicone spray. Listed prices vary widely between sellers, so compare before buying.",
