          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a snow pusher or snow shovel better?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A pusher is better on long, flat pavement when snow can be pushed to the edge, because you never have to lift. A shovel is better when snow has to go up onto a bank, on steps and in tight spaces. Many homes benefit from owning both: a pusher for open runs and a combo for everything else."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do ergonomic snow shovels really help?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "They help with lifting. Bent shafts and second handles, like the Snow Joe Shovelution's spring-assist grip, keep you more upright and shift the work from your lower back to your arms and legs. They do not make heavy loads light or twisting throws safe, so pushing where possible and taking small loads still matter most."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are wheeled snow shovels worth it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "On smooth, flat pavement with light snow, they can save a lot of strain, because the wheels carry the blade's weight. In deep, wet or packed snow they struggle, and they cannot clear steps or gravel. They are also heavy and bulky. Treat one as a second tool for the right driveway, not as your only shovel."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I buy a plastic or metal snow shovel?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Plastic with a steel wear strip is the best all-round choice: light, slick and protected at the edge. Aluminum suits heavy snow, packed ridges and very cold climates where plastic can crack. All-steel blades are heavy and mostly for ice. On decks, pavers and stamped concrete, use plain plastic to avoid scratches."
          }
        },
