          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How wide should a snow shovel be?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For lifting, 18 to 20 inches is the practical maximum for most people in wet snow. For pushing on flat pavement, 24 to 36 inches clears more per pass. Size for your heaviest typical snow: in wet-snow regions keep lifting shovels narrow, and in dry-snow regions a wider blade saves real time."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long should a snow shovel handle be?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Long enough that you can keep the blade flat on the ground without stooping. A common rule of thumb is that the top of the handle should reach around your chest. Taller people should look for longer or telescoping shafts, and a bent or second-handle design helps anyone keep a straighter back."
          }
