          <a href="#storage" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Care and Storage</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most people own the wrong snow shovel for their driveway. A narrow scoop on a long, flat drive turns a twenty-minute push into an hour of lifting, and a wide pusher on wet, heavy snow is a back injury waiting to happen. The shape of the blade matters more than the brand on the handle, so this guide starts there.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked These Shovels
          </h2>

          <p>
            A snow shovel looks too simple to need a buying guide, but the choices change the job a lot. Blade width decides how many passes a driveway takes. Blade depth decides whether you push snow or lift it. The edge decides whether the blade lasts one winter or ten, and the handle decides whether your back hurts the next morning. We sorted the market by those four things before looking at brands.
          </p>

          <p>
            We did not run our own shoveling tests for this guide, and we will not pretend otherwise. Every pick comes from published specifications: the manufacturer's product page where we could reach it, and retailer listings where we could not. Each spec in the product cards names its source in plain words. Where two listings disagreed, as they often do on weight and handle length, we used the more conservative figure and said so.
          </p>

          <p>
            We chose one shovel per job rather than ranking seven nearly identical combos. Most homes need one or two of these, not all seven. A short suburban driveway needs a good 18-inch combo. A long, flat driveway is faster with a combo and a wide pusher. A shoveler with back trouble should start with an ergonomic second-handle design. Anyone digging out cars or clearing a deck needs a scoop that can be lifted and thrown.
          </p>

          <p>
            We left out shovels whose specs we could not confirm, ones sold only under shifting marketplace brand names, and electric shovels, which have their own guide. If your driveway is long enough that shoveling is a real chore every storm, read our <a href="/electric-snow-shovel/">electric snow shovel guide</a> and our <a href="/best-electric-snow-blower/">electric snow blower picks</a> before buying a manual tool.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="shovel-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Pusher, Scoop or Combo: Pick the Shape First
          </h2>

          <p>
            There are three basic shapes, and almost every snow shovel is a variation on one of them. A <strong>pusher</strong> has a wide, shallow, curved blade designed to roll snow forward along pavement. A <strong>scoop</strong> has a narrow, deep head designed to carry a load that you lift and throw. A <strong>combo</strong> sits in between: a medium-width blade with enough curve to push and enough depth to carry.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pushers are the fastest tool on long, flat pavement because you never have to lift. You walk the blade to the edge of the driveway and tip it off. The catch is that they only work when there is somewhere to push the snow. On a narrow drive hemmed in by a fence or garden beds, snow has to go up and over, and a 36-inch blade full of snow is too heavy to throw.
          </p>

          <p>
            Scoops are slow on open pavement but unmatched when you have to lift. Decks, steps, the space around a parked car, and the end of the driveway after the plow goes by all need snow lifted and thrown. A scoop's depth also helps in deep, dry snow that would spill off the sides of a shallow blade.
          </p>

          <p>
            Combos are the compromise, and for most homes they are the right first purchase. An 18-inch combo pushes a light snowfall, carries a moderate one and throws snow onto a bank without much trouble. If you buy one shovel, buy a combo. Add a pusher for a long driveway, or a scoop if you regularly dig out cars or clear raised surfaces.
          </p>

