        <div id="blade-width" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Blade Width vs Snow Weight
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wider is faster, but only until the blade gets too heavy to move. Snow varies enormously in weight. Commonly cited figures put fresh, fluffy snow at around 5 to 7 pounds per cubic foot, while wet, heavy snow can run 15 to 20 pounds per cubic foot or more. The same blade full of snow can therefore weigh three times as much after a warm, wet storm as after a cold, dry one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Run the numbers on an ordinary 18-inch combo. Imagine a load 1.5 feet wide, a foot long and six inches deep: that is about three quarters of a cubic foot. In fluffy powder that is a load of four or five pounds. In wet snow it is twelve to fifteen pounds, held at the end of a four-foot lever. Do that a few hundred times on a driveway and it is easy to see why shoveling is hard work.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why we recommend an 18-inch combo as the default and treat wider blades as a specialty. A 24-inch combo moves a third more snow per pass, which helps in light snow and hurts in wet snow. A 36-inch pusher is excellent on a long, flat drive in powder, but in heavy snow you will find yourself pushing half-loads, which defeats the point.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical rule is to size the blade for your heaviest typical snow, not your lightest. If your region gets wet, heavy storms, stay at 18 to 20 inches for anything you lift and use a pusher only on flat runs. If you mostly get cold, dry snow, a 24-inch combo or a 36-inch pusher will save real time.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Fill the blade by weight, not by volume.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">In wet snow, take half a blade at a time. A half-full 18-inch blade of wet snow weighs about what a full one weighs in powder, and your back cannot tell the difference between a big load of light snow and a small load of heavy snow.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="materials" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Poly, Aluminum or Steel: Blade Materials and Wear Strips
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most snow shovels sold today have a plastic blade, usually polyethylene or polypropylene. Plastic is light, cheap and slick, so snow slides off it, and it will not scratch decking or pavers. Its weaknesses are edges and cold: a bare plastic edge wears down quickly on concrete, and cheap plastic can become brittle in deep cold and crack when it hits a frozen ridge.
          </p>

          <p>
            A wear strip fixes the first problem. It is a strip of steel or aluminum riveted along the front edge of a plastic blade, and it takes the abrasion so the plastic does not. It also gives you a harder edge for scraping packed snow. Our top pick, the budget pick and the ergonomic pick all have one, and we would not buy a plastic shovel for concrete or asphalt without it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Aluminum blades cost more and weigh more, but they stay stiff in any cold and scrape packed snow better than plastic. They are the right choice if you regularly deal with the frozen ridge the plow leaves at the end of the driveway, or if your shovel lives in an unheated garage in a very cold climate. The downside is that wet snow sticks to bare aluminum, so a quick coat of silicone spray before a storm makes a noticeable difference.
          </p>

          <p>
            All-steel blades are heavy and mostly belong to ice choppers and contractor tools. For ordinary homeowners, they make sense only for chipping ice, not moving snow. On decks, pavers or stamped concrete, skip metal edges entirely and use a plain plastic blade, because a steel strip can scratch or chip soft surfaces.
          </p>

