          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best type of snow shovel for most people?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                An 18-inch combo shovel with a steel wear strip suits most homes. It is narrow enough to lift a load of wet snow, curved enough to push light snow across pavement, and the strip protects the plastic edge on concrete. Add a wide pusher for a long, flat driveway, or a scoop if you often dig out cars or clear decks.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a snow pusher or snow shovel better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A pusher is better on long, flat pavement when snow can be pushed to the edge, because you never have to lift. A shovel is better when snow has to go up onto a bank, on steps and in tight spaces. Many homes benefit from owning both: a pusher for open runs and a combo for everything else.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do ergonomic snow shovels really help?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They help with lifting. Bent shafts and second handles, like the Snow Joe Shovelution's spring-assist grip, keep you more upright and shift the work from your lower back to your arms and legs. They do not make heavy loads light or twisting throws safe, so pushing where possible and taking small loads still matter most.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are wheeled snow shovels worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On smooth, flat pavement with light snow, they can save a lot of strain, because the wheels carry the blade's weight. In deep, wet or packed snow they struggle, and they cannot clear steps or gravel. They are also heavy and bulky. Treat one as a second tool for the right driveway, not as your only shovel.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a plastic or metal snow shovel?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Plastic with a steel wear strip is the best all-round choice: light, slick and protected at the edge. Aluminum suits heavy snow, packed ridges and very cold climates where plastic can crack. All-steel blades are heavy and mostly for ice. On decks, pavers and stamped concrete, use plain plastic to avoid scratches.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How wide should a snow shovel be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For lifting, 18 to 20 inches is the practical maximum for most people in wet snow. For pushing on flat pavement, 24 to 36 inches clears more per pass. Size for your heaviest typical snow: in wet-snow regions keep lifting shovels narrow, and in dry-snow regions a wider blade saves real time.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should a snow shovel handle be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Long enough that you can keep the blade flat on the ground without stooping. A common rule of thumb is that the top of the handle should reach around your chest. Taller people should look for longer or telescoping shafts, and a bent or second-handle design helps anyone keep a straighter back.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
